<p>... early 1940s photo ... as a 1940s linen postcard</p>

<p>The three-story structure was 121 ft x 130 ft with a 60 ft x 104 ft auditorium on the second floor with room to seat 3,000 boasting two balconies, a full stage and lighting. It could be configured as a full-size basketball court or as a dance floor. A second auditorium existed on the lower floor with showers and dressing rooms for both athletics and dramatics. A dedicated meeting room was also provided for the American Legion, plus other smaller common meeting rooms for other smaller organizations such as Girl and Boy Scouts.The swimming pool, 75 ft x 35 ft wide, was at the time not only the largest pool in the state but the only municipally-owned indoor pool in North Dakota. The shallow end was 2 1/2 feet deep and included a wading pool in one corner. The pool's depth sloped to 8 feet at the opposite end.The Memorial Building boasts an Art Deco poured concrete design with brick veneer popular for large public buildings in the 1920s and 1930s.</p>

